<worksheet>
    <title>
        Laplace Transform - Definition and Properties 
    </title>

    <objectives>
        <ul>
            
            <li>
                <p>
                    Introduce the concept of the Laplace transform and its basic properties.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Evaluate the Laplace transform of basic functions.
                </p>
            </li>
   
        </ul>
    </objectives>
<introduction>
    <p>
        The main idea is to use the Laplace transform to convert the constant-coefficient differential equation
for <m>y(t)</m> into a simpler algebraic equation for the Laplace-transformed function <m>Y(s)</m>,
solve this algebraic equation, and then transform <m>Y(s)</m> back into <m>y(t)</m> via the inverse Laplace transform. The correct
definition of the Laplace transform and the properties that this transform satisfies
makes this solution method possible. An exponential  is used in solving homogeneous constant-coefficient ODEs,
and the exponential function correspondingly plays a key role in defining the Laplace
transform. 

    </p>
</introduction>
<definition>
    <statement>
        <p>
            The Laplace transform of <m>f (t)</m>, denoted by
                        <m>F(s) = \mathcal{L}\left( f (t)\right)</m>, is defined by the integral
                        transform
                        <md xml:id="Lap" number="yes">
                            F(s)=\int_{0}^{\infty} e^{-st}f(t)\,dt
                        </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>
<p>
    The improper integral given by (<xref ref="Lap"/>) diverges if <m>f (t)</m>
        grows faster than <m>e^{st}</m> for large <m>t</m>.
        Accordingly, some restriction on the range of s may be required to guarantee
        convergence of (<xref ref="Lap"/>), and we will assume without further
        elaboration that these restrictions are always satisfied.
</p>
<exercise workspace="0.8in">
    <introduction>
        <p>
          Show that the Laplace transform is a <em>linear transformation</em>.
                      That is, show that for all functions <m>f_{1}(t),f_{2}(t)</m> and all constants
                      <m>c_{1},c_{2}</m>, we have
                      <md>\mathcal{L}(c_{1}\cdot f_{1}(t)+c_{2}\cdot f_{2}(t))=c_{1}\mathcal{L}(f_{1}(t))+c_{2} \mathcal{L}(f_{2}(t)).</md>  
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <solution>
        <p>
            By the definition of the Laplace transform, we have
            <md>
                \mathcal{L}(c_{1}\cdot f_{1}(t)+c_{2}\cdot f_{2}(t)) = \int_{0}^{\infty} e^{-st} (c_{1} f_{1}(t) + c_{2} f_{2}(t)) \, dt
            </md>
            <md>
                = c_{1} \int_{0}^{\infty} e^{-st} f_{1}(t) \, dt + c_{2} \int_{0}^{\infty} e^{-st} f_{2}(t) \, dt
            </md>
            <md>
                = c_{1} \mathcal{L}(f_{1}(t)) + c_{2} \mathcal{L}(f_{2}(t))
            </md>
        </p>
    </solution>
</exercise>
<example workspace="0.8in">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Compute the Laplace transform of the function <m>f(t) = 1</m>, where <m>a</m> is a constant.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <solution>
        <p>
            By the definition of the Laplace transform, we have
            <md>
                \mathcal{L}(1) = \lim_{b\rightarrow \infty}\int_{0}^{b} e^{-st} \, dt = \lim_{b\rightarrow \infty}\left[ -\frac{1}{s} e^{-st} \right]_{0}^{b} = \lim_{b\rightarrow \infty}\left( -\frac{1}{s} e^{-sb} + \frac{1}{s} \right) = \frac{1}{s}
            </md>
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
<example workspace="0.8in">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Compute the Laplace transform of the function <m>f(t) = e^{at}</m>, where <m>a</m> is a constant.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <solution>
        <p>
            By the definition of the Laplace transform, we have     
            <md>
                \mathcal{L}(e^{at}) = \lim_{b\rightarrow \infty}\int_{0}^{b} e^{-st} e^{at} \, dt = \lim_{b\rightarrow \infty}\int_{0}^{b} e^{-(s-a)t} \, dt = \lim_{b\rightarrow \infty}\left[ -\frac{1}{s-a} e^{-(s-a)t} \right]_{0}^{b} = \lim_{b\rightarrow \infty}\left( -\frac{1}{s-a} e^{-(s-a)b} + \frac{1}{s-a} \right) = \frac{1}{s-a}
            </md>
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
<example workspace="0.8in">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Compute the Laplace transform of the function <m>f(t) = t</m>.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <solution>
        <p>
            By the definition of the Laplace transform, we have
            <md>
                \mathcal{L}(t) = \lim_{b\rightarrow \infty}\int_{0}^{b} e^{-st} t \, dt = \lim_{b\rightarrow \infty}\left[ -\frac{t}{s} e^{-st} - \frac{1}{s^{2}} e^{-st} \right]_{0}^{b} = \lim_{b\rightarrow \infty}\left( -\frac{b}{s} e^{-sb} - \frac{1}{s^{2}} e^{-sb} + \frac{1}{s^{2}} \right) = \frac{1}{s^{2}}
            </md>
        </p>
    </solution>     
</example>
<exercise workspace="0.8in">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Compute the Laplace transform of the function <m>f(t) = t^{2}</m>.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <solution>
        <p>
            By the definition of the Laplace transform, we have
            <md>
                \mathcal{L}(t^{2}) = \lim_{b\rightarrow \infty}\int_{0}^{b} e^{-st} t^{2} \, dt = \lim_{b\rightarrow \infty}\left[ -\frac{t^{2}}{s} e^{-st} - \frac{2t}{s^{2}} e^{-st} - \frac{2}{s^{3}} e^{-st} \right]_{0}^{b} = \lim_{b\rightarrow \infty}\left( -\frac{b^{2}}{s} e^{-sb} - \frac{2b}{s^{2}} e^{-sb} - \frac{2}{s^{3}} e^{-sb} + \frac{2}{s^{3}} \right) = \frac{2}{s^{3}}
            </md>
        </p>
    </solution>
</exercise>
<exercise workspace="0.8in">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Compute the Laplace transform of the function <m>f(t) = t^{3}</m>.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <solution>
        <p>
            By the definition of the Laplace transform, we have
            <md>
                \mathcal{L}(t^{3}) = \lim_{b\rightarrow \infty}\int_{0}^{b} e^{-st} t^{3} \, dt = \lim_{b\rightarrow \infty}\left[ -\frac{t^{3}}{s} e^{-st} - \frac{3t^{2}}{s^{2}} e^{-st} - \frac{6t}{s^{3}} e^{-st} - \frac{6}{s^{4}} e^{-st} \right]_{0}^{b} = \lim_{b\rightarrow \infty}\left( -\frac{b^{3}}{s} e^{-sb} - \frac{3b^{2}}{s^{2}} e^{-sb} - \frac{6b}{s^{3}} e^{-sb} - \frac{6}{s^{4}} e^{-sb} + \frac{6}{s^{4}} \right) = \frac{6}{s^{4}}
            </md>
        </p>
    </solution>
</exercise>
<exercise workspace="0.8in">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Conjecture the Laplace transform of the function <m>f(t) = t^{n}</m>.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <solution>
        <p>
            By the pattern observed in the previous examples, we conjecture that
            <md>
                \mathcal{L}(t^{n}) = \frac{n!}{s^{n+1}}
            </md>
        </p>
    </solution>
</exercise>
<exercise workspace="0.8in">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Compute the Laplace transform of the function <m>f(t) = \sin(bt)</m> and <m>g(t) = \cos(bt)</m>.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <hint>
        <p>
          Note that <m>\sin(bt)=\mbox{Im}(e^{ibt})</m> and <m>\cos(bt)=\mbox{Re}(e^{ibt})</m>. Find the Laplace transform of <m>e^{ibt}</m> first, and then take the imaginary and real parts respectively.
        </p>
    </hint>
</exercise>
<exercise workspace="0.8in">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Compute the Laplace transform of the function <m>f(t) = y^\prime(t)</m>.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <hint>
        <p>
            Use integration by parts to compute the Laplace transform of <m>y^\prime(t)</m>, and the fact that <m>y(0)=y_0</m> .
        </p>
    </hint>
</exercise>
<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Compute the Laplace transform of the function <m>f(t) = y^{\prime\prime}(t)</m>.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <hint>
        <p>
            Use integration by parts to compute the Laplace transform of <m>y^{\prime\prime}(t)</m>, and the fact that <m>y(0)=y_0</m> and <m>y^\prime(0)=y_0^\prime</m> .
        </p>
    </hint>
</exercise>
<exercise workspace="0.8in">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Conjecture the Laplace transform of the function <m>f(t) = y^{(n)}(t)</m>. You may assume that <m>y(0)=y_0</m>, <m>y^\prime(0)=y_0^\prime</m>, <m>y^{\prime\prime}(0)=y_0^{\prime\prime}</m>, and so on up to <m>y^{(n-1)}(0)=y_0^{(n-1)}</m>.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    
</exercise>
    </worksheet>